an  nothing  when it comes to the vaccine  statistically we're down at the bottom  in Europe and in the world sadly we in  the Balkans are distrustful and open to  myths and fairy tales and fears and  unverified information  the young doctor here agrees even among  medical staff there are many against the  covert vaccine he says and then there's  the hesitant government  the worst for me Isn't the slow  implementation of regulations to fight  the pandemic but that our health system  is on the brink of collapse and doctors  aren't supporting it  Bulgaria's covert death rate is higher  than that of any other EU State and  clearly so is its vaccine skepticism  epidemiologist and part of the national  covet surveillance team in Bulgaria she  studied vaccine hesitancy in the country  I also say hello to Janina steinage he's  Professor for Global Health at the  Technical University in Munich hello to  both of you I would like to start with  you Savina because covet mortality rate  in Bulgaria is among the highest in the  EU only about 20 percent of the  population there are vaccinated and we  don't even know that number for sure we  don't know how many people have fake uh  certificates why are so many people  there including doctors hesitant to get  vaccinated  yes we have uh thank you we have  actually become a cautionary tale of uh  the tall vaccine hesitancy can take  um vaccine hesitancy here is not a new  problem uh Bulgaria has consistently  ranked high on vaccine hesitancy  measurements uh across the planet in  different studies the reasons are  different it depends on the  subpopulation you study it can be very  diverse but one overarching thing that  we find in our uh various interviews and  studies we've done is uh distrust and  this is deep uh distrust towards  institutions including Public Health  institutions when that distress reaches  a certain threshold you have a situation  where people will look closer to home  for advice of sources that may not be  well equipped to provide good  information so that would be relatives  friends colleagues and will be reluctant  to actually follow guidance coming from  standard expert institutions  right I mean and you know you as I  mentioned uh you were researching about  vaccination hesitance in age European  countries how does the situation compare  so in Bulgaria we definitely have the  highest level of vaccine hesitancy uh 59  in our study of men were vaccine  hesitant towards the covid-19 vaccines  and 66 of women and we see several  reasons for that based on our  qualitative data so there are five key  reasons for this vaccine hesitancy there  are fears of side effects concerns about  the potential harmful long-term  consequences of the vaccine especially  in view of the recent and quite fast  development then there are General  um concerns about the efficacy and  quality of the vaccines partly based on  the finding that vaccinated individuals  can still transmit the virus and some of  our respondents call into question the  threat posed by the pandemic or even  they even deny the existence of the  covid-19 pandemic all together and  therefore they don't see the benefits of  getting vaccinated and then lastly what  was just mentioned by my colleague one  of the key reasons is also distrust  towards political Elites and the  political system altogether and some of  our respondents refer to vaccination as  a sort of means of State control or  state oppression  so what what do we do about it I mean  there's obviously sort of false  information out there uh there is a lot  of doubt about the efficacy conspiracy  theories all of that plays a role Yanina  what would be policy conclusions that  emerge from the findings you just  mentioned  yeah it's very uh interesting this  question and we set out to test  different communication strategies so  that we can generate an evidence-based  on how we can reduce vaccine hesitancy  um but we find in our study that there's  not an outright answer we find a lot of  heterogeneity in terms of how some of  these communication strategies which  work effectively in some countries but  not in others and in Bulgaria none of  the strategies that we tested for  example one of the communication  messages tried to emphasize the risk  reduction achieved by the vaccines one  of the messages tried to emphasize  hedonistic benefits and restoration of  public life associated with vaccination  and one of the messages pointed to the  covid-19 vaccination passport that could  then give individuals certain access  rights but none of these messages proved  successful in reducing the hesitancy  level in Bulgaria so actually the main  in conclusion from our study is that  there's no  one-size-fits-all strategy for Europe as  such and that we need to carefully  tailor messages to individual countries  and better understand the worries and  concerns in each Target population and  in Bulgaria this would involve targeting  specifically women and more  disadvantaged individuals with lower  educational background and lower income  levels  our last question to you brief answer if  you could uh how do you think could this  health literacy in Bulgaria be improved  um it's really a matter of trust here  um communication is key there's no  Central uh one-size-fit-all  um solution but we do have people on the  ground even with cases and doctors we  have a lot of doctors and professionals  on the ground who are actually they know  their communities they talk to their  communities and they are well equipped  to communicate well what we need here is  uh support managerial and and a  political will to support a wide  communication strategy where we involve  those people on the ground that our best  place to help in our situation now  epidemiologist and part of the national  covet surveillance in Bulgaria and  Yanina steinert Professor for Global  Health at the Technical University in  Munich thank you so much for your time  for one of your questions now over to  Derek Williams  what's the best approach in talking to  people who have adopted conspiracy  theories about covet 19.  talking to people who've adopted  conspiracy theories especially if  they're people that you care deeply  about that can be a very difficult and  delicate matter but there are a few  approaches that pretty much all of the  experts in this field agree on the first  thing is that it's important to remain  calm even if you feel like screaming  sometimes because though it might make  you feel better for a minute or two  screaming has never convinced anyone of  anything in other words it's important  to always treat the person that you're  talking to with respect no matter how  much you disagree with their views even  if they believe in things that to you  might seem ridiculous after all you  expect that from them too right  the topic is so charged here in Germany  that the government has even published  advice in the form of some fundamental  recommendations first is something that  a lot of people forget which is to  solidly prepare for any discussion with  someone who's adopted conspiracy  theories  by informing yourself first about the  topic as thoroughly as you can the  second recommendation  is to actually talk to the person  involved don't have the conversation via  text  and have the talk in a private setting  because the goal is not to publicly  shame anyone  third is to have an open calm discussion  where you ask questions about their  opinions without acting as though you  think that those opinions are absurd or  stupid because she prepared for the  conversation in advance by doing your  own research  you should have a list of respectable  sources offered to share those sources  and talk about them at a future date  unfortunately experts in this area also  mostly agree that that once people go  down the rabbit hole it can be very hard  to get them back out again so  don't expect instant success  [Music]  and that's all for now thanks for  watching stay safe  [Music]

